property_value: function_notes "conducts the flow of bile countercurrent to the direction of portal blood flow and connects with the canal of Hering[DOI:10.1002/cphy.c120027]" xsd:string
property_value: structure_notes "The bile canaliculi merge and form bile ductules, which eventually become common hepatic duct. Hepatocytes are polyhedral in shape, therefore having no set shape or design. They have surfaces facing the sinusoids, (called sinusoidal faces) and surfaces which contact other hepatocytes, (called lateral faces). Bile canaliculi are formed by grooves on some of the lateral faces of these hepatocytes. Microvilli are present in the canaliculi but are sparse. [WP]" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0001284
name: renal column
def: "A column of tissue that is histologically identical to tissue found in the renal cortex, and which is located in the medullary region of kidney, between the renal pyramids." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1572-1316, PMID:17449786]
comment: The renal columns have been thought of as being part of the medulla only because they are located in the inner, medullary region of the kidney, rather than in the outer, cortical region. Histologically, they are extensions of the renal cortex.
