property_value: function_notes "shark liver produces copious amounts of urea, making the shark hyperosmotic to seawater, & thus a shark acts like a fresh water fish, constantly gaining water. Excess salts are concentrated by the rectal gland and secreted" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0004758
name: salt gland
def: "An organ for excreting excess salts. It is found in elasmobranchs, seabirds, and some reptiles.[WP]." [Wikipedia:Salt_gland]
subset: functional_classification
subset: organ_slim
xref: BTO:0001204
xref: GAID:1235
xref: MESH:D012491
xref: Wikipedia:Salt_gland
intersection_of: UBERON:0002530 ! gland
intersection_of: capable_of GO:0055078 ! sodium ion homeostasis
relationship: confers_advantage_in ENVO:00000447
relationship: in_taxon NCBITaxon:7742 ! Vertebrata <vertebrates>
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:40674 ! Mammalia
property_value: taxon_notes "In sharks, salt glands are found in the rectum, but in birds and reptiles, they are found in or on the skull, in the area of the eyes, nostrils or mouth. In crocodiles, the salt is excreted through the tongue. Such glands work by active transport via sodium-potassium pump that moves salt from the blood into the gland, where it can be excreted as a concentrated solution. Salt glands function to keep salt balance, and allow marine vertebrates to drink seawater" xsd:string

