[Term]
id: UBERON:0006633
name: coracoid process of scapula
def: "A process of the scapula in most mammals representing the remnant of the coracoid bone of lower vertebrates thas has become fused with the scapula." [http://www.merriam-webster.com/medical/coracoid%20process, PHENOSCAPE:ad]
synonym: "coracoid process" EXACT [Wikipedia:Coracoid_process]
synonym: "coracoid process of the scapula" EXACT [Wikipedia:Coracoid_process]
synonym: "processus coracoideus" RELATED OMO:0003011 [Wikipedia:Coracoid_process]
xref: EMAPA:25121
xref: FMA:13455
xref: SCTID:181912007
xref: Wikipedia:Coracoid_process
is_a: UBERON:0005913 {source="FMA"} ! zone of bone organ
relationship: in_taxon NCBITaxon:40674 ! Mammalia
relationship: part_of UBERON:0006849 {source="EMAPA", source="FMA"} ! scapula
property_value: depiction "http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Gray205_left_scapula_lateral_view.png" xsd:anyURI
property_value: external_definition "The coracoid process is a small hook-like structure on the lateral edge of the superior anterior portion of the scapula. Pointing laterally forward, it, together with the acromion, serves to stabilize the shoulder joint. It is palpable in the deltopectoral groove between the deltoid and pectoralis major muscles. It is the site of attachment for several structures: * The pectoralis minor muscle - to 3rd to 5th ribs. * The short head of biceps brachii muscle - to Radial tuberosity. * The coracobrachialis muscle - to medial humerus. * The coracoclavicular ligament - to the clavicle. (The ligament is formed by the conoid ligament and trapezoid ligament.) * The coracoacromial ligament - to the acromion[WP][Wikipedia:Coracoid_process]." xsd:string {source="Wikipedia:Coracoid_process"}
property_value: taxon_notes "In humans is situated on its superior border and serves for the attachment of various muscles" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0006634
name: lingual vein
def: "The lingual veins begin on the dorsum, sides, and under surface of the tongue, and, passing backward along the course of the lingual artery, end in the internal jugular vein. The vena comitans of the hypoglossal nerve (ranine vein), a branch of considerable size, begins below the tip of the tongue, and may join the lingual; generally, however, it passes backward on the Hyoglossus, and joins the common facial." [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
synonym: "deep lingual veins" RELATED [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
synonym: "lingual vein" RELATED [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
synonym: "ranine" RELATED [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
synonym: "ranine vein" RELATED [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
synonym: "vena lingualis" RELATED OMO:0003011 [Wikipedia:Lingual_veins]
