property_value: editor_note "consider distinguishing cup from predecessor of ossicle. Hall: The scleral cartilaginous cup is a distinctly sepa- rate element composed of hyaline cartilage" xsd:string
property_value: editor_note "relationship to sclera - overlaps?" xsd:string
property_value: external_definition "Bilaterally paired thin continuous band of cartilage that sagittally encircles the eye.[TAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-08-14", external_class="TAO:0001509", ontology="TAO", source="ZFIN:curator"}
property_value: taxon_notes "May be calcified in chondrichthyans. Tetrapods without this element (e.g., snakes and mammals) have a collagenous sclera. May not be homologous - see http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/17051547. cup-shaped in birds. Present in monotremes." xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0010290
name: scleral ossicle
def: "A bony plate around the eyes, are situated at the corneal-scleral limbus. They do not articulate with other elements of the skull. Typically, the scleral ossicles overlap the rim of a cup of scleral cartilage that supports the eyeball. Situated near the equator of the eye and may slightly overlap the rim of scleral cartilage." [PMID:17051547]
synonym: "scleral ossicle" EXACT [PMID:17051547]
synonym: "sclerotic bone" EXACT [ZFA:0001411]
synonym: "sclerotic ossicle" EXACT []
xref: SCTID:45632000
is_a: UBERON:0010296 ! scleral skeletal element
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:7777 {source="PMID:17051547 Walls 1942"} ! Chondrichthyes
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:7878 {source="PMID:17051547 Kemp 1999"} ! Dipnomorpha
relationship: part_of UBERON:0001697 {source="ZFA"} ! orbit of skull
relationship: part_of UBERON:0011156 ! facial skeleton
property_value: function_notes "The function of these elements is to support the eye from within the sclera; scleral ossicles play an additional important role during accommodation in terrestrial vertebrates (Lemmrich, 1931; Walls, 1942; King and McLelland, 1984)." xsd:string
property_value: taxon_notes "The distribution of scleral ossicles and scleral cartilage among verte- brate taxa is not the same. Some taxa (e.g., birds) have both elements; some only have one element (e.g., crocodiles only have a scleral cartilage); while others (e.g., snakes) have neither element (Walls, 1942) number of ossicles making up the sclerotic ring as well as the shape of both the individual plates and the sclerotic ring is variable among tetrapod taxa.... It is therefore extremely likely that scleral skeletal elements (ossicles and cartilage) of all rep- tiles (including birds) are homologous (but cannot be assumed for reptiles and teleosts)" xsd:string

